+# smallpt studio
+
+Kevin Beason's smallpt C++ path tracer, compiled to WebAssembly and displayed on a responsive canvas in `index.html`.
+
+The complete static application is checked in, including `assets/smallpt.wasm`; no dependencies or compilation are needed to use it with existing static hosting. The browser runs the renderer in a module Web Worker and receives progressive RGBA frames. Randomization changes the two sphere materials and the side walls before starting a new render. Individual colors, sample count, resolution, reset, and PNG download are also available.
+
+## Build
+
+- `npm run build` packages the static application into `dist/`.
+- `npm run build:wasm` recompiles `src/renderer.cpp` with Emscripten (`emcc` must be on PATH).
+- `npm run check` exercises the compiled WASM in Node: progressive sampling, deterministic output, visible opaque pixels, changed rendering after recoloring, and the production worker at 640 × 480.
+- `node scripts/render-poster.mjs` regenerates the startup image using the same WASM renderer at 640 × 480 and 128 samples per pixel.
+
+Serve the files over HTTP(S) using static hosting that supports `.wasm` files and JavaScript module workers. Direct `file://` browsing is not supported by browser module security. No server-side renderer, cross-origin isolation, or API keys are needed.
+
+## Source and license
+
+Original project: https://www.kevinbeason.com/smallpt/
+
+`vendor/smallpt.cpp` is Kevin Beason's unmodified original source, licensed under MIT; see `vendor/LICENSE.txt`. The adapter retains the original scene, intersection, reflection, refraction, and radiance functions. The build allocates a 16 MiB stack for the original recursive radiance function. It replaces the command-line image loop with an exported progressive sampler and an RGBA framebuffer. Each pass adds one sample per 2 × 2 subpixel (four samples per pixel). Random seed and material colors are passed from the UI. Emscripten's generated JavaScript loads the compiled WebAssembly.
+
+`assets/initial-render.png` is a real render generated from this WASM, used as a startup preview while the live renderer initializes. All subsequent frames are computed client-side.
+
+Validation was performed with command-line checks and a static build. No development server or browser verification was used.
